+// GPU GS backend (OpenGL 4.5 + ARB_fragment_shader_interlock, Windows).
+//
+// The GPU rasterizes; everything the GS does per pixel (texture lookup, alpha
+// and depth tests, blending, write masks) runs in a fragment shader on integer
+// values, inside an ordered interlock, with the same maths as
+// GSCpuBackend::WritePixel. Colour and Z live in R32UI images, one per render
+// target, so results are bit-comparable with the CPU backend.
+//
+// The CPU backend is kept as the owner of GS local memory: uploads, transfers,
+// the CLUT and presentation all still go through it. Render targets are mirrored
+// on the GPU and written back to local memory only when something reads them
+// (presentation, a transfer, a texture that isn't the target itself), and
+// reloaded when something else writes their pages.
